<TopicPage slug="subjects/welding/foundational/welding-safety-first">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Welding is one of the few trades where the process itself radiates, fumes, spatters and
    shocks — so the discipline begins with safety, not as a formality but as the first
    professional skill. Every welding curriculum (AWS SENSE included) opens here, and every
    incident report explains why.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four hazards and the systems that tame them" widget="equationStep" config={hazardsCfg} />

  <h2>UV burns arrive without warning</h2>
  <p>
    The arc's ultraviolet output causes photokeratitis — "arc eye" — hours after exposure, with
    the sensation of sand under the eyelids. There is no warming glow to warn you, which is why
    helmet shade selection (typically shade 10–13 for stick at working amperages) and full
    coverage are enforced from the first lesson. Bystanders at ten metres can be burned too:
    screens protect everyone in sight of the arc.
  </p>

  <h2>Fume: the engineering control</h2>
  <p>
    Welding fume is a respirable metal-oxide aerosol whose composition depends on the rod, the
    base metal and any coatings — galvanised steel adds zinc oxide (metal fume fever), stainless
    adds hexavalent chromium (a recognised carcinogen). The control hierarchy is unambiguous:
    capture at the source with local exhaust ventilation, then respiratory protection. Welding in
    a confined space without extraction is the trade's textbook fatal scenario.
  </p>

  <h2>Fire watch: after the arc, not during</h2>
  <p>
    Sparks and slag travel far further than the weld — through floor cracks, into rags, along
    conduits — and smoulder. The standard radius for clearing combustibles is about 11 metres
    (35 feet), plus blankets where clearing is impossible, plus a fire watch that remains for at
    least 30 minutes after work stops. The permit system (hot work authorisation) exists because
    the fires that happen are almost always the fires nobody watched.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AWS SENSE program guidelines; ANSI Z49.1, <em>Safety in Welding, Cutting and Allied Processes</em>; OSHA 1910.252 hot work provisions.</p>
</TopicPage>
